**Compare**: the gain is Uo = Ui + RF × Ui / RG = (1 + 22/10) Ui = **3.2 Ui**.
- 10%: Ui ≈ 0.90 V → Uo ≈ **2.88 V**, and IN− is 0.90 V too (virtual short);
- 20%: Ui ≈ 1.80 V → Uo ≈ **5.76 V**; the current in RG = 1.80 / 10k = 0.18 mA, and RF carries the same 0.18 mA;
- 50%: Ui = 4.50 V → 14.4 V in theory, but the output can only reach V+ − 1.5 V = **7.5 V**, so it is clipped (saturated). Now IN− = 7.5 × 10 / 32 ≈ 2.34 V, no longer equal to IN+: the virtual short fails in saturation.
